# t-launcher add-on index: what each add-on is, what it needs, what to know first.
# install.sh copies it to ~/.local/share/t-launcher/INDEX. t-launcher, install.sh and
# cleankey all read their add-on list from it.
#
# This file is DATA. The launcher reads it line by line and never
# sources, evals or runs anything in it. Unknown fields are ignored,
# a malformed block is skipped, and a missing file falls back to a
# built-in name list. Installed add-ons with no block here still show
# in the menu ("no description yet") and still run.
#
# One block per add-on, blank line between blocks.
#
# Required
#   name        command name (letters, digits, dash; matches addons/<name>)
#   short       menu blurb, 40 characters or less
#   long        the description strip, about 3 lines on a phone
#   level       new | comfortable | power
#               SUGGESTED starting level only. Never restricts access:
#               every add-on is searchable and installable at every level.
# Optional
#   example     one thing to try first
#   pkgs        Termux packages it uses (shown before install)
#   perms       Android permissions it will ask for
#   apps        companion Android apps that must be installed
#   hardware    physical gear it is for
#   background  yes = this add-on CAN run something that keeps running.
#               It is a label, not permission. Installing never starts
#               it; the user is asked separately, with a plain reason.
#   warning     shown before install and before first run, for add-ons
#               whose actions have significant consequences

name: tdoctor
short: check & fix Termux
long: Checks your Termux setup and offers to fix what it finds. Good first stop when something breaks.
example: tdoctor --quick
level: new

name: cleankey
short: safe cleanup + undo
long: Shows what's safe to delete in home, ~/bin and Download, and why. Deletes go to a trash you can undo.
example: cleankey why ~/somefile
level: new
pkgs: fzf
perms: storage

name: recentkey
short: recent files
long: Quickly return to files you've used without searching through folders. Keeps your last 20.
example: MENU > Recent files
level: new
pkgs: fzf

name: clipkey
short: clipboard history
long: Remembers what you copy so you can get an old clip back. "clipkey clear" wipes the history.
example: clipkey
level: new
pkgs: fzf, termux-api
apps: Termux:API
background: yes

name: offset
short: pipe offset & grade
long: Pipe offset and grade math in feet and tenths: run, diagonal, rolling offsets, fall over a distance.
example: offset 2.4 45
level: new

name: dropwatch
short: auto-handles downloads
long: Watches Download and handles new files for you: scripts become commands (never run), APKs get scanned. Every change has Undo.
example: dropwatch rules
level: comfortable
pkgs: termux-api
perms: storage
apps: Termux:API (for the Undo button)
background: yes
warning: Moves and installs files from Download on its own once the watcher is running.

name: netkey
short: what's on your Wi-Fi
long: Shows every device on your Wi-Fi and helps you work out what each one is. Speed test too.
example: netkey
level: comfortable
pkgs: nmap, curl, fzf, termux-api
apps: Termux:API
warning: Only scan networks you own or have permission to scan.

name: talk
short: voice commands
long: Say what you want and it finds the matching command, then asks before running it. Teach it your own phrases with "talk add".
example: talk list
level: comfortable
pkgs: termux-api
perms: microphone
apps: Termux:API
warning: Speech gets misheard. talk shows the command and asks before running it, except for commands that only open a basic tool menu. Read the prompt before you say yes.

name: cardkey
short: Cardputer over Wi-Fi
long: Runs an M5Stack Cardputer from your phone: browse its SD card, send files, flash firmware, make backups.
example: cardkey
level: comfortable
pkgs: curl, fzf
hardware: M5Stack Cardputer running Launcher
warning: Flashing firmware replaces what is on the Cardputer.

name: sukey
short: near-root (Shizuku)
long: ADB-level powers via Shizuku, no root. Some actions change how the whole phone behaves, not just Termux.
example: sukey help
level: power
apps: Shizuku (set up and running)
warning: Can remove or freeze apps, grant permissions and change phone-wide Android settings. Read each prompt before you say yes.
